{"step_index":640,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-22T00:09:09Z","thinking":"**Analyzing Potential Errors**\n\nI've discovered `output_file` is assigned `None`, which converts to `null` in JSON. I'm now exploring the implications of the JavaScript `JSON.parse(item.shortage_files || '[]')` call. Since `shortage_files` is a JSON string representing a list, `JSON.parse` should work, but a null might lead to an unintended result.\n\n\n**Reviewing `forEach` Logic**\n\nI'm now focusing on the `loadHistory()` function within `index.html`. Specifically, I'm examining how the `forEach` loop iterates through the `history` data. It seems I should also look at the handling of the `status` field, determining how different values are translated into visual cues and interactive behavior for the user. I'll need to explore how the card's onclick handler works when the status is not 'processing'.\n\n\n**Investigating `delete_process` Errors**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the UI thread errors reported by `pywebview` during `deleteProcess` calls. It is strange that `delete_process` doesn't do anything with the UI! I am puzzled by the interaction. Since the user found two records in processing, I am trying to determine if there is a race condition in the `delete_process` or how it interacts with the UI. I will look at how `loadHistory` refreshes the UI.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Dialog Interactions**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. I realized `deleteProcess` uses `confirm()`, a JavaScript blocking native dialog. This is critical. In WebView2, `confirm()` can trigger `CoreWebView2Controller` errors within `pywebview` on Windows. The dialog hijacks the message pump, potentially causing thread access issues. The errors I've seen align with this perfectly.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"python -c \"import re; f=open('d:/AI_Engineer/Pharmacy-agy/desktop_app/templates/index.html', encoding='utf-8'); print('deleteProcess' in 